1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,155
Why does Canada have so many lakes?

2
00:00:02,505 --> 00:00:05,001
Fly over northern Canada and you'll see

3
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:07,753
something strange. The land looks like it's

4
00:00:07,753 --> 00:00:10,057
full of holes, thousands of lakes in

5
00:00:10,057 --> 00:00:11,081
every direction.

6
00:00:11,431 --> 00:00:14,036
A global study in 2016 counted about

7
00:00:14,036 --> 00:00:16,714
one point four million lakes on Earth

8
00:00:16,714 --> 00:00:20,333
bigger than ten hectares. Around sixty-two percent

9
00:00:20,333 --> 00:00:22,794
of them are in Canada. That's more

10
00:00:22,794 --> 00:00:25,255
than every other country combined.

11
00:00:25,605 --> 00:00:28,061
The reason is ice. During the last

12
00:00:28,061 --> 00:00:29,939
ice age, a sheet of ice up

13
00:00:29,939 --> 00:00:33,117
to three kilometers thick covered almost all

14
00:00:33,117 --> 00:00:33,839
of Canada.

15
00:00:34,189 --> 00:00:36,543
As that ice moved, it scraped the

16
00:00:36,543 --> 00:00:39,395
land like a giant bulldozer, carving out

17
00:00:39,395 --> 00:00:41,677
millions of hollows in the rock.

18
00:00:42,027 --> 00:00:44,617
Then, around ten thousand years ago, the

19
00:00:44,617 --> 00:00:47,401
ice melted. The water filled those hollows,

20
00:00:47,401 --> 00:00:49,409
and lakes were born everywhere.

21
00:00:49,759 --> 00:00:52,162
Much of Canada sits on the Canadian

22
00:00:52,162 --> 00:00:54,771
Shield, some of the oldest and hardest

23
00:00:54,771 --> 00:00:57,381
rock on the planet. Water can't easily

24
00:00:57,381 --> 00:00:59,372
soak into it or wear it away,

25
00:00:59,372 --> 00:01:00,745
so the lakes stayed.

26
00:01:01,095 --> 00:01:03,389
Canada also shares four of the five

27
00:01:03,389 --> 00:01:06,339
Great Lakes with the United States. Together,

28
00:01:06,339 --> 00:01:08,305
they hold about a fifth of the

29
00:01:08,305 --> 00:01:10,141
world's fresh surface water.

30
00:01:10,491 --> 00:01:13,478
And the biggest lake entirely inside Canada,

31
00:01:13,478 --> 00:01:15,855
Great Bear Lake, is bigger than the

32
00:01:15,855 --> 00:01:17,552
whole country of Belgium.

33
00:01:17,902 --> 00:01:19,590
So next time you look at a

34
00:01:19,590 --> 00:01:21,667
map of Canada full of blue dots,

35
00:01:21,667 --> 00:01:24,719
remember: you're looking at the fingerprints of

36
00:01:24,719 --> 00:01:25,433
an ice age.

37
00:01:25,783 --> 00:01:28,535
Many Canadian lakes have no official name

38
00:01:28,535 --> 00:01:30,749
at all. There are simply too many

39
00:01:30,749 --> 00:01:32,897
to name, so maps often show them

40
00:01:32,897 --> 00:01:34,508
as nameless blue shapes.

41
00:01:34,858 --> 00:01:37,514
Some provinces even use their lakes as

42
00:01:37,514 --> 00:01:40,589
a slogan. Manitoba's old license plates said

43
00:01:40,589 --> 00:01:43,315
land of one hundred thousand lakes, and

44
00:01:43,315 --> 00:01:45,482
the real number is even higher.

45
00:01:45,832 --> 00:01:48,350
The same ice also shaped the northern

46
00:01:48,350 --> 00:01:51,616
United States. That's why Minnesota calls itself

47
00:01:51,616 --> 00:01:53,725
the land of ten thousand lakes.

48
00:01:54,075 --> 00:01:56,003
Would you rather live by a lake

49
00:01:56,003 --> 00:01:57,683
or by the ocean? Tell me in

50
00:01:57,683 --> 00:01:58,491
the comments.

